TITLE
Uniform Dataflow

CHAPTER
02.cad

ABSTRACT

Synchronous dataflow (SDF) is well suited to multi-rate signal processing applications. In SDF, actors have static firing rules: they consume and produce a fixed number of data tokens in each firing. This model lends itself to efficient, static scheduling, avoiding the runtime scheduling overhead incurred by general implementations of process networks. SDF is based on token consumption and production rates of actors to solve the balance equations and deadlock problems. For some executable models, SDF fails to find the executable schedule. [1] Cyclo-static dataflow (CSDF) could resolve the schedule with exposing the internals about the actors. CSDF is a generalization of SDF, whose actors have cyclically changing firing rules. The benefits of CSDF over SDF are simpler precedence constraints and exposure of internal details via cyclic firing rules. [2]
<p>
Dynamic dataflow (DDF) lets the firing rule be any Boolean expression which is undefined at compile time. The runtime scheduling mechanism determines which actors are executable. The limitations of DDF include the loss of deterministic behaviors, runtime overhead and possibly unbounded buffers. The key point of the scheduling algorithm of DDF is that all executable actors fire once and only once during each minor iteration.
<p>
Our approach is to generalize the SDF, CSDF and DDF as Uniform dataflow (UDF). The UDF model consists of hierarchically mixed SDF/CSDF models. A UDF schedule can be generalized from an SDF model. The method begins with computing the SDF schedule of the SDF model based on the balance equations. Then apply the DDF scheduling algorithm on the SDF schedule to construct a UDF schedule. From this UDF schedule, cyclic firing rules for the UDF model can be derived. The cyclic firing rules expose more details about the internals of the UDF model while still providing modularity. Because the schedule calculation of CSDF is similar to SDF, a composite UDF model can be clustered from several UDF models via the similar method presented above.
<p>
A lot of interesting interactions could be studied based on the cyclic firing rules. One of them is to analyze the model composition, e.g. the schedulability analysis of UDF models caused by the change of the topology. The key and difficult issue is how to generate the abstract yet detailed enough cyclic firing rules from the UDF schedule.  Other interesting topics include causality analysis, parallel architecture design and the relationship to the timed dataflow. 
<p>
For implementation on real architectures, our exploration on UDF is focused on these four properties: periodicity, bounded execution time, bounded memory and no deadlock.
<p>
Reference:
<p>
